--- title Calling a function with arguments in hand
--- about APPLY reaches a function when the arguments are already a
--- about block rather than written after the call, and FORSKIP walks a
--- about series in strides. ASSERT is the one that raises rather than
--- about answering.

--- id applying/calling-a-native-with-a-block-of-arguments
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
apply :add [1 2]
--- result
3

--- id applying/calling-a-user-function-the-same-way
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note A caller cannot tell the two apart, which is the point.
--- code
f: func [a b] [a + b]
apply :f [1 2]
--- result
3

--- id applying/applying-with-too-few-arguments
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note The arity is still checked, so a short block is the same
--- note mistake as a short call.
--- code
apply :add []
--- error script expect-arg

--- id applying/an-assertion-that-holds-answers-true
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
assert [true]
--- result
#(true)

--- id applying/an-assertion-of-an-expression
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
assert [1 = 1]
--- result
#(true)

--- id applying/an-assertion-that-fails-raises
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note With an id of its own rather than a generic script error, so a
--- note caller can tell an assertion from anything else that went
--- note wrong.
--- code
assert [false]
--- error script assert-failed

--- id applying/equal-values-hash-alike
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note The only thing worth pinning about HASH. The number itself is
--- note the implementation's business, and pinning R3's would mean
--- note reimplementing R3's hash function for no gain.
--- code
b: [1 2 3]
c: [1 2 3]
(hash b) = (hash c)
--- result
#(true)

--- id applying/different-values-hash-differently
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
(hash "a") = (hash "b")
--- result
#(false)
